{
  "language_code": "eng",
  "language_probability": 1.0,
  "text": "Orbit hangs in the dark, a tiny orange figure against a star that looks almost ordinary. Then the numbers arrive and ordinary dies. If you drifted toward a neutron star, the last thing your body would feel is not falling. Space itself would try to tear you into a line of atoms, and your eyes would see light bend into a ring before you could blink. So what actually happens to you second by second as the distance closes? What would you see? What would you feel? And at what point does you stop being a useful word? This began as a giant. A star many times heavier than our sun burned through its life too fast, too hot, too hungry. When the fuel in its heart ran out, the core collapsed. The outer layers blasted away in a supernova, a brief, furious goodbye. What remained was not ash. What remained was an argument gravity almost won",
